.TH WPS 1 "@DATE@" "WPS @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wps \- WPS for scripts: objects, messages and questions, in plain text
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wps open
.IR PATH ...
.br
.B wps get
.I PATH NAME
.br
.B wps set
.I PATH
.RI ' NAME = VALUE ;...'
.br
.B wps create
.I FOLDER CLASS TITLE
.br
.B wps shadow
.I PATH FOLDER
.br
.B wps say
.RB [ \-\-stay ]
.I TEXT
.br
.B wps notify
.I TITLE
.RI [ TEXT ]
.br
.B wps ask
.I TEXT
.RI [ BUTTON ...]
.br
.B wps folder
.br
.B wps selection
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wps
lets scripts work with WPS's objects, as REXX scripts did on OS/2. Its
output is plain text, one value a line; it exits with 0 when it worked,
1 when it did not (with a message on standard error).
.TP
.B open
Opens each object as a double click would: a folder's window, a file in
its program, a program.
.TP
.B get
Prints a property's value (for example
.BR "wps get ~/Work icon_font" ).
.TP
.B set
Sets properties with a setup string. The path may also be
.BI class: NAME
(every object of a class) or
.BI type: MIME
(every file of a type).
.TP
.B create
Makes an object of a class (WPFolder, WPDataFile, ...) in a folder and
prints its path.
.TP
.B shadow
Makes a shadow of an object in a folder and prints its path, or its
address (\fBvobj:\fIID\fR) when the folder keeps shadows centrally.
.TP
.B say
Shows a message at the bottom of the desktop; with
.B \-\-stay
it stays until clicked.
.TP
.B notify
Shows a notification.
.TP
.B ask
Shows a WPS dialog with the buttons given (OK if none) and prints the one
chosen; exits with 1 if it was closed without a choice.
.TP
.B folder
Prints the folder the script or Command Prompt was started from
.RB ( $WPS_FOLDER ).
.TP
.B selection
Prints the paths of the objects selected in the active folder window
(else on the desktop).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WPS_FOLDER
Set by Command prompt here: the folder.
.TP
.BR WPS_OBJECT ", " WPS_PATH ", " WPS_EVENT
Set for programs an object's actions start.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wpsctl (1),
.BR wps-terminal (1),
.BR wps-shell (1)
